sx - simple xinit replacement
=============================
sx is a simple alternative to both xinit(1) and startx(1) for starting
an Xorg server.

It started life as a proof of concept while attempting to learn how both
xinit and startx worked while taking up the offer presented in startx:

    > Site administrators are STRONGLY urged to write nicer versions.

It is not a direct replacement as it provides a different, minimalist
interface. This fork adds optional server command-line customization via an
xsvrc file while preserving upstream behavior.

In this document, CONFIG refers to ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/sx.


Features
--------
* Configurable server command via CONFIG/xsvrc.
* Server listens only on UNIX domain sockets by default.
* Server starts on the same TTY the user logged in on.
* First DISPLAY is 1 instead of 0, coupled directly to the TTY number.
* Server uses -noreset by default.
* Minimal error checking, letting individual tools handle their own output.
* No dependence on /etc/X11/xinit infrastructure.
* Ignores XINITRC and .xinitrc in favor of executable CONFIG/sxrc.


XSVRC
-----
If CONFIG/xsvrc exists, it is sourced instead of running the default command.
If absent, sx runs:

    exec Xorg :"$tty" vt"$tty" -keeptty -noreset -nolisten tcp

The file is sourced by sh (no shebang or executable bit needed). $tty is
available and must be used for display and vt arguments.

Example CONFIG/xsvrc:

    exec Xorg :"$tty" vt"$tty" -keeptty -noreset -nolisten tcp \
        -background none -quiet -logverbose 0 -verbose 0

Rules:
* Command must begin with `exec`, otherwise $server PID will point to the
  wrapping subshell instead of Xorg, breaking cleanup (`kill "$server"`).
* `-keeptty` is required; `-noreset` is strongly recommended.
* Do not add trailing `)` or `&`.
* Note that `-extension NAME` disables an extension; `+extension NAME`
  enables it. Disabling XTEST breaks tools such as xdotool.


Requirements
------------
* Xorg


Installation
------------
PREFIX defaults to /usr/local; override it on the command line if needed.

Run the following command to install sx:

    make PREFIX=/usr install


